🌹 Scarlet
Building a Full-Stack Flower Shop App
Workshop – Full Stack Apps with AI
Practical workshop for the course 'Full Stack Apps with AI' — build a multi-platform full-stack flower shop platform with Next.js, React, PostgreSQL and Expo, using Git and AI dev agents.

Technology Stack
Layer	Technology
Back-end	TypeScript + Next.js 14 + Drizzle ORM + PostgreSQL (Neon)
Front-end	TypeScript + Next.js + React + Tailwind CSS
Mobile app	React Native + Expo
AI	Google Gemini 2.0 Flash — plant health analysis & species ID
Storage	Cloudflare R2 (S3-compatible) for photos & product images
i18n	next-intl (web) · i18next (mobile) — Bulgarian default + English
Deployment	Netlify (web) · EAS (mobile)

 
Project Description: Scarlet
Build Scarlet -- a boutique flower shop platform with an embedded AI-powered plant health tracking feature targeting Bulgarian users (bilingual BG/EN).

The app has two core domains: (1) a flower shop where customers can browse and order flowers, and (2) a personal plant collection tracker with AI health analysis.

Roles in the App
•	Visitor: can view the home page, browse the flower catalog, and register.
•	User: can manage own profile, personal plant collection, view orders, and use the AI plant scan.
•	Admin (optional): can manage the plant species catalog, flower products, and user accounts.

Visitors
Visitors are anonymous users who visit the Scarlet website.
•	Visitors can see the home page, browse the flower catalog, and register (by email + password).
•	Visitors can view the public plant species catalog.

Registered Users
Registered users have a profile with name, email and photo (optional) and can login / logout.
•	Users can maintain a personal plant collection (add, edit, remove plants).
•	Each plant can have a photo, health score, care stats, and an optional link to the species catalog.
•	Users can request an AI plant health analysis (up to 5 per day).
•	Users can browse and order flowers from the shop.

Plant Collection & AI Analysis
The core feature of Scarlet — users track their plants and get AI-powered health insights.
•	Plants table — personal plant owned by a user. Holds health score, care stats, photo, and optional species link.
•	Plant Species Catalog — global reference database of 500+ species with bilingual names (BG/EN), care guides, and scientific info.
•	AI Scan (Quick Scan) — ad-hoc photo upload to analyze an unknown plant without saving it first.
•	AI Analysis (Saved Plant) — deep analysis of a saved plant: health score 0–100, issues with severity, care recommendations.
•	Rate limit: 5 analyses per user per 24 hours (DB-enforced).
•	AI provider: Google Gemini 2.0 Flash via @google/generative-ai.

Flower Shop
The shop section lets customers browse and purchase flowers.
•	Product catalog with categories, prices, and photos (stored in Cloudflare R2).
•	Users can add items to a cart and place orders.
•	Order history visible in the user profile.

Web App and Mobile App
•	Web app — the primary app. Implements full functionality: auth, user profile, plant collection, AI scan, flower shop, orders.
•	Mobile app — scope-limited companion app. Implements: login / register, view personal plants, AI quick scan, view active orders.

 
Project Requirements
Capstone project requirements for all students.

Technologies
•	Backend: Next.js 14 App Router + Neon serverless PostgreSQL + Drizzle ORM.
•	Frontend: Next.js + React + TypeScript + Tailwind CSS.
•	Mobile: React Native + Expo (Expo Router).
•	AI: Google Gemini 2.0 Flash for plant analysis.
•	Storage: Cloudflare R2 for photos and product images.
•	i18n: next-intl (web) and i18next (mobile) — Bulgarian default, English secondary.
•	Deployment: Netlify (web) + EAS (mobile).

Architecture
•	Client-server architecture: React frontend + Next.js backend (Server Actions for web, RESTful API for mobile).
•	Node.js monorepo: apps/web (Next.js) + apps/mobile (Expo) + packages/shared (TypeScript types & Zod validators).
•	Structure business logic in a service layer consumed by Server Actions and the RESTful API.
•	Modular design: split into self-contained UI pages, components, services, route handlers, and utils.
•	Define an AGENTS.md file with agent instructions, architectural guidelines, technology standards, and project-wide conventions.

User Interface (UI)
•	Implement a modern, user-friendly UI with a warm floral aesthetic.
•	Implement responsive design for desktop and mobile browsers.
•	Split UI into reusable components and sub-components.
•	Use icons, effects and visual cues (e.g. plant health badges, seasonal flower highlights).
•	Use server-side components in Next.js by default; add 'use client' only when needed.

Backend
•	Use Neon DB (serverless PostgreSQL) accessed via @neondatabase/serverless HTTP driver.
•	Use Drizzle ORM for schema migrations and DB access — never raw SQL.
•	Implement business logic as a service layer, consumed by Server Actions and API routes.
•	Implement a RESTful API for the mobile app.
•	Implement server-side paging to prevent performance issues for large datasets.
•	Use Cloudflare R2 for all user-uploaded photos and product images.

Authentication and Authorization
•	Use bcrypt / argon2 for secure password hashing.
•	Use JWT tokens: access tokens (15 min) + refresh tokens (7 days). Web uses httpOnly cookies; mobile uses expo-secure-store + Bearer header.
•	Use withAuth(handler, options) wrapper for all protected API routes.
•	Never expose password_hash in API responses.

Database
•	Design the PostgreSQL schema with normalization, relationships, and indexing.
•	Always use Drizzle Kit migrations when changing the schema.
•	Seed enough sample data (plants, species, products, orders) to ensure realistic testing.

i18n
•	Bulgarian (bg) is the default language.
•	Translation keys live in apps/web/src/messages/bg.json and apps/web/src/messages/en.json.
•	Mobile keys mirror web keys in apps/mobile/src/i18n/bg.json and apps/mobile/src/i18n/en.json.
•	Use namespace prefixes: nav.*, auth.*, plants.*, catalog.*, shop.*, admin.*, ai.*, common.*
•	All text visible to users must use useTranslations() (web) or useTranslation() (mobile) — no hardcoded strings.

Deployment
•	Deploy the Next.js web app live on the Internet via Netlify.
•	Deploy the Expo app as a Web export via EAS / Netlify.
•	Provide sample credentials (e.g. demo@scarlet.com / demo123) to simplify testing.
•	Configure all environment variables: DATABASE_URL, JWT secrets, R2 credentials, GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY.

GitHub Repo
•	Use a GitHub repo to hold all project assets.
•	Commit and push each successful change during development.
•	Generate project documentation in the README (description, architecture, DB schema, setup guide).

 
Steps to Build the Project
Follow these steps (or similar) to build the app incrementally.

Create a VS Code Project
Create a new project workspace in VS Code, e.g. scarlet.

Define the Repo Structure
Use a monorepo holding the following project assets:
•	package.json → dependencies and scripts for the entire repo
•	apps/web/ → Next.js Web app (back-end + front-end)
◦	apps/web/src/app/ → front-end client app
◦	apps/web/src/app/api/ → backend API endpoints
◦	apps/web/src/services/ → app business logic
◦	apps/web/src/lib/db/ → DB schema and scripts
◦	apps/web/src/lib/ai/ → AI plant analyzer (plant-analyzer.ts)
◦	apps/web/src/drizzle/ → Drizzle ORM migrations
◦	apps/web/src/messages/ → i18n translation files (bg.json, en.json)
•	apps/mobile/ → Expo mobile app
◦	apps/mobile/src/ → Expo mobile app code
◦	apps/mobile/src/i18n/ → mobile translation files
•	packages/shared/ → shared TypeScript types and Zod validators (User, Plant, Product, Order, ...)

Steps to follow:
1.	Initialize the monorepo: npm init
2.	Initialize a new Next.js app in apps/web/:  npx create-next-app@latest apps/web
3.	Initialize a new Expo app in apps/mobile/:  npx create-expo-app@latest apps/mobile
4.	Create packages/shared to hold shared types: mkdir -p packages/shared/src
5.	Create npm run build and npm run dev scripts in the root project.

Setup GitHub Repo
Commit and push the project into a GitHub repo.
•	Discard any nested sub-project Git repos; initialize one new monorepo at the root.
•	Configure .gitignore (exclude node_modules, .env.local, .next, dist).
•	Restart VS Code after creating the monorepo to reload Source Control correctly.
•	Publish the project to GitHub.

Setup AGENTS.md Instructions
Write an AGENTS.md instructions file to provide app context and guidelines for the AI dev agent.
Define 3 different AGENTS.md files:
•	AGENTS.md (root) — agent instructions for the entire project (web + mobile):
◦	Brief project description: Scarlet — boutique flower shop + plant health tracker
◦	Back-end, front-end, mobile apps
◦	Folder structure
•	apps/web/AGENTS.md — agent instructions for the Next.js Web app:
◦	Scarlet: flower shop + plant collection tracker (users browse flowers, manage plants, run AI scans)
◦	Technologies: Next.js 14 + Neon DB + Drizzle ORM + React + Tailwind + Gemini AI + next-intl
◦	Architectural guidelines: service layer, modular design, auth (JWT + httpOnly cookies), database
◦	UI guidelines: floral aesthetic, responsive design, server-rendered components, bilingual (BG/EN)
•	apps/mobile/AGENTS.md — agent instructions for the Expo mobile app:
◦	Scarlet mobile: view personal plants, AI quick scan, view active orders
◦	Technologies: Expo, React Native, Expo Router, i18next
◦	Back-end: Scarlet RESTful API, with Bearer token auth
◦	Mobile UI guidelines: user-friendly UI, stack navigation, responsive layout, native alert fallbacks for Web

 
Steps to Build the Next.js Project

Create Neon DB
Create a new project in Neon DB, e.g. ScarletDB.
Configure the Neon DB connection settings in .env.local:
DATABASE_URL=<your_db_url_from_neon.com>
GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY=<your_gemini_api_key>
JWT_SECRET=<random_secret>
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=<random_refresh_secret>
R2_BUCKET_URL=<cloudflare_r2_url>

Design the Database
Design the initial database schema. Key tables:
•	users (email, password_hash, name, photoUrl) — app users
•	plants (user_id, name, photo_url, health_score, care_stats, species_id?) — personal plant collection
•	plant_species (name_bg, name_en, scientific_name, family, care_guide, difficulty) — global species catalog
•	plant_stats
•	ai_analyses (plant_id?, user_id, photo_url, result_json, created_at) — AI health analysis results
•	products (name_bg, name_en, category, price, photo_url, stock) — flower shop products
•	orders (user_id, status, total, created_at) — customer orders
•	order_items (order_id, product_id, quantity, price) — items in each order

Use simple numbers as IDs for table rows.
Install libraries: Drizzle ORM, Drizzle Kit, @neondatabase/serverless, dotenv, @google/generative-ai.
Create the Drizzle schema file, then generate and run migrations:
npm run db:generate
npm run db:migrate

Seed Sample Data
Create a script npm run db:seed to seed sample data.
Create sample users:
◦	admin@scarlet.com / admin123 (admin role)
◦	demo@scarlet.com / demo123 (regular user)
◦	maria@scarlet.com / pass123
◦	ivan@scarlet.com / pass123
Create sample plant species (500+ entries from a CSV or AI-generated list).
Create sample plants for demo user (5–10 plants with photos and health scores).
Create sample flower products (roses, tulips, orchids, etc.) with prices and photos.
Insert sample orders for the demo user.
Insert sample AI analyses for demo user plants.
npm run db:seed

Public Pages and Layout
Create the public pages of the app:
•	Home Page — hero section with Scarlet branding, featured flowers, and register / login buttons.
•	Login Page — login form (client component). Put in route group (auth).
•	Register Page — registration form (client component). Put in route group (auth).
•	Flower Catalog — public product listing, filterable by category.
•	Plant Species Catalog — public bilingual species browser (BG/EN).
•	Define the app layout: header, main, footer. Implement responsive layout for the header.

Login / Logout
•	Implement auth with JWT: access tokens (15 min) in httpOnly cookies + refresh tokens (7 days).
•	Keep user passwords hashed with bcrypt.
•	Implement server-side auth logic as Server Actions.
•	Show the currently logged-in user in the site header.
•	When no user is logged in, show [Login] | [Register] links.
•	Protect all routes except home, catalog, and auth pages to require logged-in users.

User Dashboard
Create the User Dashboard page: /dashboard
•	Section My Plants-- list all users plants as cards (photo, name, health score badge, last analysis date).
•	Section My Plants-- list all users plants as cards (photo, name, health score badge, last analysis date).
•	Section My Plants-- list all users plants as cards (photo, name, health score badge, last analysis date).
•	Display [Dashboard] link in the header navigation for logged-in users.
•	After user login, redirect to the User Dashboard.

Plant Collection
Create pages for managing the user's personal plant collection:
•	/plants — list all user's plants
•	/plants/new — add a new plant (name, photo upload, optional species link)
•	/plants/[id] — view plant detail (health history, AI analyses, care tips)
•	/plants/[id]/edit — edit plant info
•	Trigger an AI analysis from the plant detail page (POST /api/plants/[id]/ai-analysis).
•	Display health score with a visual indicator (0–100 scale, color-coded).
•	Show identified issues with severity levels and care recommendations.

AI Quick Scan Page
Create a dedicated /scan page for ad-hoc AI plant scanning:
•	User uploads a photo (or takes one on mobile) of any plant.
•	The app sends the photo to POST /api/ai/quick-scan.
•	Display the AI result: species identification (common name BG/EN, scientific name, family, care difficulty) + health assessment (score, issues, recommendations).
•	Rate limit: 5 analyses per user per 24 hours, enforced in the DB.
•	The result is stored in the ai_analyses table and linked to the user.
•	User can optionally save the identified plant to their personal collection.

Flower Shop
Create the flower shop pages:
•	/shop — flower product catalog with category filter and search
•	/shop/[id] — product detail page (photo, description, price, add to cart)
•	/cart — shopping cart
•	/orders — user order history

 
Minimalistic RESTful API
Build a RESTful API in the Next.js project for mobile app clients. Start from basic functionality.

Auth	POST /api/auth/login — login by email + password → return JWT access token

Auth	POST /api/auth/refresh — refresh JWT access token using refresh token

Plants	GET /api/plants — list user's plants (with JWT auth, with paging)

Plants	GET /api/plants/[id] — plant detail (name, photo, health score, species, AI analyses)

AI	POST /api/plants/[id]/ai-analysis — run AI health analysis on a saved plant

AI	POST /api/ai/quick-scan — ad-hoc AI scan from a photo (no plant ID required)

Shop	GET /api/products — list flower products (with paging, category filter)

Orders	GET /api/orders — list user's orders (with JWT auth)

Catalog	GET /api/species — list plant species catalog (public, with paging, BG/EN search)

Docs	GET /api/docs — display API documentation as HTML (needed for the Expo app)

 
Steps to Build the Expo Project
Build the Expo mobile app with core functionality: login, view plants, AI quick scan, view orders.

Add API Docs to AGENTS.md
Add API docs URL to apps/mobile/AGENTS.md:
# API Docs
Back-end API documentation: http://localhost:3000/api/docs
Back-end API source code: ../apps/web/src/app/api

Home Page
Build the app layout, navigation and home page:
•	Empty the project — remove all template pages, styles, themes, and example assets.
•	Create empty screens: Home | Login | Plants | Plant Detail | Scan
•	Implement stack navigation between screens.
•	Home page — welcome message with Scarlet branding + login link.

Login / Logout
Configure the RESTful API URL in .env:
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000/api
Build login / logout functionality using the API endpoints:
•	Implement a login form with error handling.
•	Store JWT access token securely using expo-secure-store.
•	Implement a [Logout] button on the home screen.
•	Make all app screens (except Home and Login) require a logged-in user.
•	Fix CORS policy errors in the backend if needed.

Plants Screen
Implement the Plants screen:
•	List user's plants (with paging) as scrollable cards.
•	Display plant photo, name, health score badge.
•	Clicking a plant card opens the Plant Detail screen.

Plant Detail Screen
Implement the Plant Detail screen:
•	Display full plant info: name, photo, health score, care stats, species info.
•	Display latest AI analysis result (score, issues, recommendations).
•	[Run AI Analysis] button — triggers a new analysis for the saved plant.

AI Quick Scan Screen
Implement the AI Quick Scan screen:
•	Photo upload or camera capture (using expo-image-picker).
•	Send photo to POST /api/ai/quick-scan and display results.
•	Show: species identification (BG/EN name, scientific name, care difficulty) + health score + issues.
•	Option to save the identified plant to the user's collection.

 
Deploy the Next.js App to Netlify
Deploy the Next.js app from GitHub to Netlify.
•	Connect the GitHub repo to Netlify.
•	Set Base Directory: apps/web
•	Configure all environment variables: DATABASE_URL, JWT_SECRET, JWT_REFRESH_SECRET, GOOGLE_AI_API_KEY, R2_BUCKET_URL, R2_ACCESS_KEY, R2_SECRET_KEY.
•	Deploy and verify all API endpoints are reachable.

Deploy the Expo App to Netlify
Deploy the Expo app from GitHub to Netlify (as a Web export).
•	Configure EXPO_PUBLIC_API_BASE_URL to point to the deployed Next.js API URL.
•	Deploy the Expo Web export.
•	Optionally build an Android APK via EAS and publish it in GitHub Releases.

 
Advanced Web App Functionality
Advanced functionality for the Next.js web app, to be built as next steps.

Dashboard Paging
Implement server-side paging for plants and orders in the User Dashboard to prevent UI freezing.

Plant Species Catalog — Admin
Implement admin-only pages to manage the plant species catalog:
•	/admin/species — list all species
•	/admin/species/new — add new species
•	/admin/species/[id]/edit — edit species details
•	Optionally use AI to auto-enrich species data (care guides, native regions) in bulk.

Flower Shop Management — Admin
Implement admin-only pages to manage flower products:
•	/admin/products — list all products
•	/admin/products/new — add new product with photo upload
•	/admin/products/[id]/edit — edit product details and stock

AI Analysis History
Implement a dedicated history page for AI analyses:
•	/plants/[id]/analyses — list all AI analyses for a plant, ordered by date.
•	Display health score trend over time as a simple chart.
•	Allow users to compare analyses side by side.

Plant Care Reminders
Implement care reminders based on AI recommendations:
•	Users can set watering, fertilizing, and repotting reminders for each plant.
•	Reminders stored in a plant_reminders table.
•	Display upcoming reminders on the User Dashboard.

 
Advanced Mobile App Functionality
Additional functionality for further work on the mobile app.

Order Placement
•	Extend the RESTful API to support browsing flower products and placing orders.
•	Implement a simple shop screen with product listing and cart.

Push Notifications
•	Use Expo Push Notifications to remind users of plant care tasks.
•	Triggered server-side when a reminder is due.

Registration
•	Add user registration to the mobile app.
•	Extend the RESTful API to support user registration.

 
Performance Test
Implement a performance test with large datasets:
•	Seed the database with 500 plant species.
•	Seed the database with 5,000 plants across the first 10 users.
•	Seed the database with 3,000 users.
•	Seed the database with 10,000 flower products.
•	Test the app UI to identify where the UI hangs or works slowly.
•	Implement paging, DB indexes and other optimizations.


